Subject: [PATCH 3/3] fs/read_write: route fd 0/1 writes to printk for early guest console

Routes userspace write(2) on fd 0 and fd 1 directly to printk, bypassing the tty
layer entirely, so that a statically-linked init's printf() reaches the serial
console without a working console device.

CAVEATS -- this is a debugging shortcut, not a design:
  * panic() on count >= 256 turns an oversized write into a dead guest.
  * copy_from_user()'s return value is discarded.
  * fd 0 is stdin; writes to it are also swallowed.

This may become unnecessary now that CONFIG_DEVTMPFS provides /dev/console and
the kernel is booted with console=uart8250. Dropping it is tracked as a
follow-up experiment; see README "Migration notes".

---
--- a/fs/read_write.c
+++ b/fs/read_write.c
@@ -637,6 +637,15 @@
 {
 	struct fd f = fdget_pos(fd);
 	ssize_t ret = -EBADF;
+	char char_buf[256];
+	
+	if (fd == 0 || fd == 1) {
+		if (unlikely(count >= 256)) panic("too many characters\n");
+		copy_from_user(&char_buf, buf, count);
+		char_buf[count] = '\0';
+		printk("%s", &char_buf);
+		return count;
+	}
 
 	if (f.file) {
 		loff_t pos, *ppos = file_ppos(f.file);
